  "worked_solution": "The denominator is $1+2\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $1-2\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $1-2\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1+2\\text{i}} & =\\frac{3}{1+2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1-2\\text{i}}{1-2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1-2\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6\\text{i}}{1-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6\\text{i}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3-6\\text{i}}{5}=(\\frac{3}{5})+(-\\frac{6}{5})\\,\\text{i}\n$$",
